  "statusText": "The regular U.S. cash session closed at fresh records roughly ninety minutes ago -- the S&P 500 finished at 7,798.99 (+0.65%), its first close above 7,800, and the Nasdaq Composite gained 0.81% to 26,803.03, both lifted by a flat July PPI print that undershot the +0.2% consensus and reinforced Wednesday's benign CPI. The VIX closed at 14.45, a seven-month low, and the 10-year Treasury yield eased more than 5bp to about 4.641% on the soft inflation data; WTI crude held its recent slide near $81-82/bbl. Alpha Vantage's free-tier daily request budget was fully exhausted this run (two consecutive calls, including one retry, returned rate_limit errors before any data was retrieved), so this assessment relies entirely on cross-confirmed web research rather than the API feed, and Confidence is held lower than last hour to reflect that. Applied Materials, a Nasdaq-100 semiconductor-equipment constituent, reported fiscal Q3 results just after the 20:00 UTC close: revenue beat consensus and the company raised its 2026 equipment-growth outlook above 30%, yet shares whipsawed roughly 4-5% lower in initial after-hours/premarket-tagged trading, consistent with the stock's history of falling on 6 of its last 8 earnings reports despite beats. This remains a contained, single-name reaction with no confirmed broad Nasdaq-100 or NQ-futures follow-through identified in current research, so Tail holds low at 15%. Stress eases slightly to 37% as the AMAT move continues to look contained and cross-asset signals (VIX, yields, oil) stay benign, though the unresolved Iran/Hormuz standoff (Tehran maintaining a threat to close the strait, Israel continuing operations in southern Lebanon) and Friday's Retail Sales/cash-open/UMich cluster keep Stress in the moderate band rather than dropping further. No new kinetic escalation or fresh market-transmission event has been identified in the geopolitical situation since the prior run.",
